import React from 'react'
type S={heading:string;text:string[]};type G={title:string;sections:S[]}
const guides:Record<string,G>={
"workout-volume-calculator":{title:"Workout Volume Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What the calculator measures",text:["Training volume is not one single physiological quantity. This tool reports session volume load from sets × reps × external load × exercise count, then scales that value by weekly frequency to estimate weekly tonnage. It also counts weekly sets and compares that count with goal-specific MEV and MRV bands implemented by the calculator.","Keep the outputs separate. A tonnage figure describes external work moved, while weekly set count is a programming proxy. Neither number directly measures muscle growth, strength adaptation, fatigue, technique quality or proximity to failure."]},
{heading:"Volume load and tonnage",text:["For the entered workout, volume load is sets multiplied by repetitions, weight and number of exercises. If you perform 4 sets of 10 reps with 60 kg across 5 exercises, the modeled session load is 12,000 kg. At three sessions per week the model reports 36,000 kg, or 36 tonnes, of weekly tonnage.","This is useful for comparing like-for-like training blocks, but tonnage can become misleading across different exercises. Moving 100 kg in a squat is not physiologically equivalent to moving 100 kg in a calf raise, machine movement or partial-range exercise. Exercise selection and range of motion remain outside the formula."]},
{heading:"Weekly sets, MEV and MRV",text:["The calculator computes weekly sets as sets per exercise × exercises per session × sessions per week. It then compares that total with fixed goal bands: the current implementation uses different minimum-effective-volume and maximum-recoverable-volume thresholds for strength, hypertrophy and endurance.","MEV and MRV are coaching concepts, not universal biological constants. Real recoverable volume varies by muscle group, training age, exercise choice, effort, sleep, calories, injury status and individual response. The tool applies one total-set band, so do not interpret its green zone as a personalized prescription for every muscle."]},
{heading:"Sets are not all equal",text:["A hard set performed close to muscular failure usually creates a different stimulus and fatigue cost from an easy warm-up set. The calculator does not ask for repetitions in reserve, RPE, tempo, range of motion or whether a set is a compound or isolation movement. Those missing variables explain why identical set counts can produce different outcomes.","For hypertrophy planning, use the weekly-set result as a bookkeeping aid. Track which muscle actually receives the work and whether performance, soreness and recovery support adding volume. For strength, specificity and heavy practice may matter more than maximizing total tonnage."]},
{heading:"Goal-specific rep ranges",text:["The result panel shows target rep and rest ranges selected by the training-goal input. Strength emphasizes lower repetitions and longer rest; hypertrophy uses a moderate rep range; endurance uses higher repetitions and shorter rest in the current model.","These ranges are practical defaults rather than hard borders. Muscle can grow across a broad loading range when sets are sufficiently challenging, while strength is strongly influenced by practice with heavier loads. Use the goal selection to frame programming, not to declare every set outside the displayed range ineffective."]},
{heading:"Progressive overload",text:["Volume should be interpreted alongside progression. If the same exercise, technique and range of motion improve from 60 kg for 10 reps to 65 kg for 10 reps, volume load rises and performance has progressed. Progress can also occur through extra repetitions, additional sets, better execution or greater range of motion.","Do not increase every variable simultaneously just to make the tonnage number larger. A sustainable progression keeps enough consistency to reveal whether performance is improving while controlling fatigue."]},
{heading:"Recovery and deloads",text:["The calculator flags set counts above its MRV threshold because excessive volume can outpace recovery. Useful real-world warning signs include declining performance across multiple sessions, persistent soreness, worsening sleep, reduced motivation and joint irritation. One difficult workout alone does not prove overtraining.","When recovery deteriorates, reducing sets, exercise redundancy or frequency can be more useful than merely lowering load. A deload is a temporary reduction in training stress; it should be driven by the training block and recovery state rather than automatically triggered by one calculator number."]},
{heading:"Calories burned estimate",text:["The tool also displays an estimated calorie value derived from volume load. Treat this as a rough model output, not a metabolic measurement. Energy expenditure from resistance training depends on body size, exercise selection, work-to-rest ratio, session duration and post-exercise effects that cannot be inferred reliably from kilograms moved alone.","Do not use the calorie estimate to precisely replace exercise calories with food or to compare resistance training with cardio. The programming outputs—sets, repetitions, load and weekly work—are the stronger use case for this calculator."]},
{heading:"How to compare training blocks",text:["Record a baseline week using consistent exercise definitions. Then compare blocks by muscle group, movement pattern and goal rather than only by grand-total tonnage. If weekly sets rise while performance and recovery remain good, the added work may be productive. If tonnage rises only because you substituted movements with mechanically larger loads, the comparison may not represent more useful stimulus.","A good training log pairs calculator outputs with exercise names, load, reps, effort and notes on recovery. That turns the number into longitudinal evidence instead of a one-off score."]},
{heading:"Common mistakes",text:["Common errors include entering pounds while the calculator is set to kilograms, counting warm-ups as hard work, multiplying every exercise as though it trained the same muscle, and treating MEV/MRV as validated personal thresholds. Another mistake is chasing volume while technique and load progression deteriorate.","The tool converts pounds to kilograms internally before computing volume. Confirm the unit selector before comparing a new result with an older training log."]},
{heading:"Practical interpretation",text:["Use this calculator to answer concrete questions: How much external work did this session contain? How many working sets am I accumulating per week? How did those numbers change when frequency or exercise count changed? Those are measurable programming questions.","Do not use it to conclude that a particular tonnage guarantees hypertrophy, strength or calorie expenditure. Training response still has to be verified through performance, body measurements where relevant, recovery and consistent practice over time."]},]},
"cobra-vs-marketplace-calculator":{title:"COBRA vs ACA Marketplace Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What the calculator compares",text:["This calculator compares premium cost under two post-employment coverage paths: continuing an employer plan through COBRA and buying an ACA Marketplace plan. It starts with the monthly premiums you enter, then optionally estimates a Marketplace premium-tax-credit effect from household income and household size.","The result is deliberately premium-focused. It does not prove which health plan is better because deductibles, coinsurance, out-of-pocket maximums, provider networks and drug formularies can outweigh a premium difference."]},
{heading:"COBRA premium mechanics",text:["COBRA generally allows eligible people to continue the same employer-sponsored group coverage after certain qualifying events. The major cost shock is that the former employee may have to pay the portion previously paid by the employer, plus permitted administrative cost, so the full premium can be much higher than the payroll deduction seen while employed.","Enter the actual COBRA election notice premium when available. Using the old employee contribution will understate continuation cost."]},
{heading:"Marketplace premium input",text:["The Marketplace premium field should represent the plan premium being compared before the calculator's modeled subsidy. Real Marketplace prices vary by location, age, household members, tobacco rules where applicable and plan selection. The benchmark plan used for federal tax-credit calculations may not be the same plan you ultimately buy.","For an actionable comparison, obtain current quotes rather than carrying forward a prior year's premium."]},
{heading:"How the subsidy model works",text:["The current implementation calculates household income as a percentage of the 2025 HHS poverty guideline for the 48 contiguous states and DC, because prior-year poverty guidelines are relevant to the modeled 2026 credit year. It then applies a simplified contribution-percentage schedule and subtracts the modeled annual credit from the entered Marketplace premium.","This is not a Form 8962 engine. Alaska and Hawaii guidelines, benchmark-plan details and the full set of Premium Tax Credit eligibility rules are outside the simplified formula."]},
{heading:"Income and household size",text:["Premium-tax-credit calculations use tax-household concepts, not simply the number of people living at an address. Household income can also differ from salary because the relevant ACA tax definition incorporates modified adjusted gross income rules.","If income is uncertain after a job loss, test more than one plausible annual-income figure. A Marketplace advance credit based on an estimate can later be reconciled on the tax return."]},
{heading:"Coverage eligibility matters",text:["The calculator includes a subsidy-eligible toggle, but switching it to Yes does not establish legal eligibility. Access to other qualifying coverage, tax filing circumstances, immigration status and other rules can affect the credit. Use the toggle only after checking the actual Marketplace/IRS rules that apply to the household.","Likewise, COBRA eligibility and duration depend on the qualifying event and plan circumstances. The page's short description should not be treated as a universal duration rule."]},
{heading:"Deductibles and out-of-pocket exposure",text:["A cheaper monthly premium can come with a larger deductible or different coinsurance. Compare expected annual premium plus expected cost sharing, and also compare the worst-case annual premium plus the plan's applicable out-of-pocket maximum. This is especially important for people with planned procedures, expensive prescriptions or ongoing specialist care.","The calculator currently reports premium savings only, so these medical-spending differences must be evaluated separately."]},
{heading:"Provider network and prescriptions",text:["COBRA can preserve the employer plan's existing network, which may be valuable during active treatment. Marketplace plans can use narrower networks, different referral rules and different formularies. Confirm physicians, hospitals and medications directly with the plan rather than assuming a carrier name guarantees identical access.","Continuity can justify a higher premium in some situations; in others, Marketplace savings dominate. The calculator gives the premium arithmetic needed to quantify that tradeoff."]},
{heading:"Worked comparison",text:["Suppose COBRA costs $700 per month and the entered Marketplace premium is $550. Before any modeled credit, annual premiums are $8,400 and $6,600, a $1,800 Marketplace advantage. If the calculator estimates a credit, the displayed Marketplace net premium falls further.","Now add plan design. If the Marketplace option has a materially larger deductible and you expect substantial care, part or all of the premium advantage could disappear. That is why annual savings is a starting point, not the final coverage decision."]},
{heading:"Enrollment timing",text:["Coverage decisions are deadline-sensitive. COBRA election rules and Marketplace special-enrollment opportunities do not operate identically, and voluntarily dropping one form of coverage may not always create the enrollment opportunity a person expects. Verify dates before allowing coverage to lapse.","When comparing options, record the date employer coverage ends, the COBRA election information and the Marketplace effective date. Avoid comparing prices without confirming whether coverage periods align."]},
{heading:"Best use of this calculator",text:["Use the tool to establish the premium gap and to see how an income assumption changes the simplified Marketplace estimate. Then build a second comparison with deductible, out-of-pocket maximum, network, prescriptions and expected care.","For tax-credit eligibility and current plan pricing, rely on official Marketplace and IRS information. The calculator is a planning model, not an eligibility determination or insurance recommendation."]},]},
"visual-acuity-risk-calculator":{title:"Visual Acuity Risk Calculator",sections:[{heading:"What this calculator actually estimates",text:["The Visual Acuity Risk Calculator is an eye-health risk screener built from age, daily screen time, daily outdoor time, time since a comprehensive eye examination and additional risk factors presented by the page. It produces a risk score, an exam-frequency suggestion and a digital-eye-strain interpretation. It does not measure visual acuity in the clinical sense of a Snellen or logMAR eye-chart test.","That distinction matters: a person can read 20/20 yet have glaucoma risk, retinal disease, dry eye or significant eye strain, while another person may have correctable refractive blur with otherwise healthy eyes. Use the score as a prompt to think about risk and preventive care, not as a substitute for an eye examination."]},{heading:"Age and changing eye risk",text:["Age changes the probability of several eye conditions and also changes focusing ability. Presbyopia commonly affects near focus in midlife, while cataract, glaucoma and retinal conditions become more common with increasing age. The calculator uses age as a broad risk signal but cannot determine whether an individual has any of these conditions.","A sudden visual change should never be attributed to normal aging based on the score. New flashes, a shower of floaters, a curtain-like shadow, sudden loss of vision, severe eye pain or neurologic symptoms require urgent assessment because timing can matter."]},{heading:"Screen time and digital eye strain",text:["Long screen sessions can contribute to digital eye strain through sustained near focus, reduced blink rate, glare, poor ergonomics and dry-eye symptoms. Common complaints include burning, dryness, headache, blurred near vision and difficulty refocusing after prolonged work. The screen-hours input is therefore a workload indicator rather than proof that screens are damaging the retina.","Breaks, appropriate viewing distance, larger text, glare control and deliberate blinking can improve comfort. The familiar 20-20-20 approach—periodically looking at a distant object—can be a useful reminder to interrupt continuous near work, although no single break schedule fits everyone."]},{heading:"Outdoor time and myopia",text:["Outdoor time is especially relevant to myopia development in children and adolescents. Population evidence links more time outdoors with a lower risk of developing myopia, although progression and treatment decisions are more complex once myopia is established. The calculator's outdoor-hours field is a broad behavioral factor, not a treatment prescription.","For adults, outdoor time should not be interpreted as a way to reverse established refractive error. Children with progressive myopia may need professional monitoring and, in selected cases, evidence-based myopia-control strategies. The tool cannot measure axial length, cycloplegic refraction or progression rate."]},{heading:"Eye examinations",text:["Time since the last comprehensive eye exam matters because many eye conditions can be asymptomatic early. A comprehensive exam can assess visual acuity, refraction, eye pressure when appropriate, ocular health and other findings based on age and risk. The recommended interval varies with symptoms, diabetes, family history, contact-lens use and prior findings.","The calculator's exam-frequency output is therefore a general planning suggestion. Someone with known glaucoma, retinal disease, diabetes-related eye findings or other diagnosed conditions should follow the schedule set by their eye-care professional rather than a generic interval generated online."]},{heading:"Glaucoma and family history",text:["Glaucoma can damage the optic nerve gradually and may not cause noticeable central-vision symptoms until substantial loss has occurred. Risk is influenced by age, intraocular pressure, family history, ancestry, corneal factors and other clinical findings. A web calculator cannot measure eye pressure or inspect the optic nerve.","If the page asks about family history or other risk factors, enter them accurately because they change the context for preventive exams. A low score is not a glaucoma screening result. Only appropriate clinical testing can evaluate the disease."]},{heading:"Diabetes and retinal health",text:["Diabetes can affect retinal blood vessels, and risk is related to duration of diabetes, glucose control, blood pressure and other factors. Visual symptoms may be absent even when retinal changes are present, which is why recommended dilated retinal examinations matter. The calculator can flag risk context but cannot see the retina.","People with diabetes should use their clinician's eye-exam recommendations rather than waiting for the online score to become high. Sudden blur can also have causes unrelated to diabetic retinopathy, including refractive changes, medication effects or acute eye disease."]},{heading:"Interpreting the risk score",text:["The 0-to-100 result is a model score created from the calculator's selected inputs; it is not a percentage probability of losing vision. If the score moves from 30 to 45 after changing screen time, that means the model assigned more risk points under that scenario, not that a disease probability increased by fifteen percentage points.","This is why the component outputs—screen exposure, outdoor time, age and exam recency—are more actionable than the exact number. Use the score to identify neglected preventive factors and to compare consistent scenarios."]},{heading:"Corrective lenses and acuity",text:["Glasses and contact lenses correct refractive error but do not by themselves indicate whether the eyes are healthy. An outdated prescription can cause blur or strain, while new blur can also reflect dry eye, cataract or other causes. The calculator does not accept prescription strength and cannot estimate whether a lens correction is appropriate.","Do not use screen-distance tricks or squinting to self-diagnose refractive error. If distance or near vision has changed, an acuity and refraction assessment provides information the risk score cannot."]},{heading:"Practical use and limitations",text:["Use the calculator periodically when habits or risk factors change, not repeatedly in search of a perfect score. Reduce uninterrupted near-work time when symptoms occur, maintain reasonable outdoor activity where appropriate, and keep eye examinations current for your risk profile. Those actions are more meaningful than small numerical changes.","The tool cannot diagnose glaucoma, cataract, retinal detachment, macular disease, amblyopia or other eye conditions. Persistent symptoms or any sudden loss of vision require professional assessment. The score is educational risk organization, not a clinical visual-acuity measurement."]},]},
"interest-rate-calculator":{title:"Interest Rate Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What this calculator solves",text:["Most loan calculators start with an interest rate and solve for payment. This tool works in reverse: given principal, monthly payment and number of months, it numerically finds the annual nominal rate that makes the standard fixed-payment amortization formula match the payment you entered.","That makes it useful when a lender quotes payment and term but you want to infer the rate embedded in those cash flows."]},
{heading:"The amortization equation",text:["For a fixed-rate amortizing loan, payment equals principal × r × (1+r)^n divided by ((1+r)^n − 1), where r is the monthly rate and n is the number of payments. Because r appears several times and inside an exponent, solving directly for r is inconvenient.","The implementation uses repeated numerical narrowing to find the monthly rate whose calculated payment matches the entered payment, then annualizes that rate for display."]},
{heading:"Minimum feasible payment",text:["If the monthly payment is no greater than principal divided by the number of months, it cannot amortize a positive-interest loan over that term. The page therefore warns when the payment is at or below the zero-interest payment.","For example, a $24,000 balance over 48 months requires $500 per month even at 0% interest. A quoted payment below $500 implies that at least one assumption—principal, term, balloon payment or other cash flow—is missing."]},
{heading:"Nominal rate versus true APR",text:["The result is labeled APR on the page, but the underlying calculation uses principal, payment and term only. A regulatory APR can incorporate certain finance charges and fees, so a loan with origination fees may have a true disclosed APR higher than the rate inferred from payment on the stated principal.","Use the output as the amortization rate implied by the entered cash flows unless all APR-relevant fees are already reflected consistently in the amount financed."]},
{heading:"Monthly rate",text:["The calculator also displays the monthly rate, approximately the annual nominal rate divided by 12. This is the periodic rate used in the amortization equation. It should not be confused with simply dividing total interest by principal and years, which ignores the declining balance.","Interest on an amortizing loan is charged against the remaining balance, so the borrower does not owe the original principal for the entire term."]},
{heading:"Total interest and total paid",text:["Total paid is monthly payment multiplied by the number of payments. Total interest is that total minus the entered principal. These figures assume every scheduled payment is made as entered and there are no extra payments, late fees, skipped payments or balloon balance.","They are useful for understanding the dollar cost of the loan even when two offers have similar monthly payments."]},
{heading:"Worked example",text:["Suppose you borrow $25,000 and pay $500 monthly for 60 months. Total scheduled payments equal $30,000, so the simple dollar difference is $5,000. The calculator then solves for the rate that produces a $500 payment on a $25,000, 60-month amortizing balance.","Changing only the term can materially change the inferred rate relationship and total interest, which is why principal, payment and months must describe the same loan contract."]},
{heading:"Why payment comparisons can mislead",text:["A lower payment is not automatically a cheaper loan. Extending the term can reduce the required monthly amount while increasing the number of interest-bearing months. The page's sensitivity chart helps show how payment changes across rates for the same principal and term.","When shopping, compare rate, fees, term and total cost together rather than optimizing only the monthly payment."]},
{heading:"Fees, add-ons and financed products",text:["Origination fees, dealer add-ons, warranties, insurance products or closing costs can distort a reverse rate calculation if the principal entered does not match the amount actually financed. A cash price of $25,000 is not necessarily a $25,000 loan if fees are rolled into the balance.","Use the financed principal from the contract when the goal is to infer the loan's periodic interest rate."]},
{heading:"Variable-rate and irregular loans",text:["The formula assumes one fixed periodic rate and level monthly payments. Adjustable-rate mortgages, teaser rates, interest-only periods, balloon loans, biweekly schedules and loans with irregular cash flows need different modeling.","If a contract changes rates over time, one rate inferred from the full payment stream can hide those mechanics and should not be treated as the contractual rate."]},
{heading:"How to verify a lender quote",text:["Enter the amount financed, scheduled payment and exact number of payments from the disclosure. Compare the calculator's inferred rate with the stated note rate, then investigate differences such as fees, timing or a final balloon payment. Small rounding differences are normal; large gaps usually indicate that the cash-flow assumptions are incomplete.","For borrowing decisions, the lender's formal disclosures and contract control. This calculator is an independent mathematical check, not a substitute for those documents."]},]},
"mime-type-lookup":{title:"MIME Type Lookup Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What a MIME type identifies",text:["A MIME type describes the media format of a resource using a type/subtype pair such as text/html, image/png, application/json, or application/pdf. Browsers, mail clients, APIs, CDNs, and servers use this metadata to decide how content should be interpreted. The lookup is therefore about protocol metadata, not about converting the file itself.","A correct extension can still be served with the wrong Content-Type header, so inspect the response when debugging web delivery."]},
{heading:"Extensions and media types",text:["File extensions and MIME types are related conventions, but they are not identical. One media type can be associated with several extensions, and an extension can have historical or vendor-specific mappings. Modern registrations maintained by IANA are the strongest reference when interoperability matters.","Treat a lookup result as a mapping aid rather than proof of the bytes inside a file."]},
{heading:"HTTP Content-Type",text:["In HTTP, the Content-Type response header tells the recipient what representation was sent. Serving CSS as text/plain, JavaScript with an incompatible type, or JSON as HTML can trigger browser errors, blocked execution, incorrect downloads, or security problems.","When a browser says a resource was blocked because of its MIME type, check the server response header first."]},
{heading:"Text and character encoding",text:["Text media types may also carry a charset parameter, for example text/html; charset=utf-8. The media type identifies the format while the character set identifies how bytes map to characters. UTF-8 is the dominant web encoding, but legacy data can still require explicit handling.","A charset problem can produce garbled text even when the MIME type itself is correct."]},
{heading:"JSON, XML, and APIs",text:["APIs commonly exchange application/json, while XML formats use application/xml, text/xml, or more specific +xml registrations. Structured suffixes such as +json and +xml indicate that a specialized format retains the processing model of JSON or XML.","Clients should validate the actual API contract instead of assuming every text-looking response is JSON."]},
{heading:"Images, audio, and video",text:["Media formats have specific types such as image/jpeg, image/webp, audio/mpeg, and video/mp4. The subtype describes the encoded format, not merely the visual or audible category. Container formats can hold different codecs, so MIME metadata alone does not describe every playback requirement.","For upload validation, combine declared type checks with safe server-side inspection rather than trusting a browser-supplied filename."]},
{heading:"Downloads and Content-Disposition",text:["Content-Type explains what a response is; Content-Disposition can influence whether it is displayed inline or offered as a download and can provide a filename. These headers solve different problems. Changing a MIME type solely to force a download can create incorrect metadata.","Use Content-Disposition: attachment when download behavior is the real requirement."]},
{heading:"Security considerations",text:["MIME confusion can become a security issue when untrusted content is interpreted as executable HTML or script. Browsers also use protections such as X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff to reduce unsafe content sniffing. Upload systems should store untrusted files carefully and serve them with deliberate headers.","Never use an extension-to-type lookup as the only malware or upload-safety control."]},
{heading:"Common debugging workflow",text:["Start with the requested URL, inspect the network response, note status code, Content-Type, Content-Disposition, and any browser console message, then compare the returned type with the file that was expected. Server configuration, reverse proxies, object storage metadata, and CDN caches can each be the source of a wrong header.","Clear or invalidate cached metadata after correcting an origin configuration."]},
{heading:"Using this lookup well",text:["Use the tool to find a likely media type from an extension or to understand a type you encountered in headers or configuration. Then confirm unusual or high-impact mappings against the current registry or the documentation for the platform receiving the file.","The useful result is a standards-aware starting point for configuration and debugging, not a claim about file contents."]},
]},
"villain-name":{title:"Villain Name Generator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What makes a villain name work",text:["A memorable villain name signals character before the audience knows the full backstory. It may suggest power, menace, elegance, corruption, technology, magic, or absurdity. The best choice depends on genre and tone rather than on how threatening a word sounds in isolation.","A comic-book archvillain, a gothic antagonist, and a satirical office villain need very different naming logic."]},
{heading:"Tone before vocabulary",text:["Decide whether the character is frightening, theatrical, mysterious, tragic, campy, or humorous. Dark words can feel unintentionally silly in a realistic thriller, while an understated surname may be too plain for a flamboyant superhero setting.","Judge a generated name inside one sentence of the story to test its tone."]},
{heading:"Titles and epithets",text:["Forms such as The Warden, Doctor Vex, Lady Ash, or the Iron Regent use titles to imply status or mythology. Titles work especially well when the antagonist has a public persona, organization, cult, office, or inherited role.","An epithet can also evolve after an important event in the character’s history."]},
{heading:"Sound and rhythm",text:["Hard consonants can make a name feel abrupt, while long vowels and multi-syllable forms can sound aristocratic or uncanny. Rhythm matters when a name will be spoken repeatedly. Alliteration is memorable but becomes cartoonish if every character uses it.","Read the candidate aloud beside the protagonist’s name and important place names."]},
{heading:"Connect name to motive",text:["A strong antagonist is more than an evil label. A name becomes richer when it relates to the character’s method, ideology, origin, profession, obsession, or public image. A finance-themed criminal, ancient sorcerer, rogue AI, and corrupt monarch should not draw from the same word bank.","Use the generator as a spark, then connect the result to a concrete character fact."]},
{heading:"Avoid accidental copies",text:["Generated combinations can resemble existing fictional characters, brands, usernames, or public figures by coincidence. Search a final name before publishing a commercial project, especially if it is unusually distinctive.","Similarity is a reason to revise, not evidence that a random generator granted exclusive rights."]},
{heading:"Building variants",text:["When a result is close but not right, vary one dimension at a time: title, noun, adjective, spelling, language influence, or level of formality. Keeping the core image while changing the surface form often produces a stronger identity than repeatedly generating unrelated names.","Save a shortlist and compare it after the character profile is clearer."]},
{heading:"Names for different genres",text:["Fantasy can support archaic titles and invented phonetics; science fiction can use callsigns, corporate designations, or machine-like identifiers; crime fiction often benefits from plausible human names and earned nicknames; comedy can deliberately exaggerate.","Genre expectations are tools, not rules, but breaking them should feel intentional."]},
{heading:"Worldbuilding consistency",text:["A villain name should belong to the same linguistic and cultural world as the rest of the cast unless being foreign to that world is part of the story. Naming systems can encode family, rank, geography, species, faction, or historical era.","Consistency makes even an invented name feel more believable."]},
{heading:"Using the generator",text:["Generate several candidates, mark the images or sounds you like, and refine rather than accepting the first output automatically. The tool is best for ideation: authors still decide whether the final name fits characterization, audience, setting, and originality needs.","The strongest final choice should still make sense after the novelty of the random result wears off."]},
]},
"random-name-generator":{title:"Random Name Generator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What random naming is good for",text:["Random names are useful for fiction drafts, games, test data, placeholder records, brainstorming, and situations where a neutral prompt is faster than inventing a name from scratch. Randomness creates variety, but it does not understand the cultural or narrative context of a project unless that context is explicitly built into the source lists.","Treat generated names as candidates, not verified identities or demographic predictions."]},
{heading:"Randomness and source lists",text:["Most simple name generators select items from predefined first-name and surname lists. The apparent diversity therefore depends more on the quality and breadth of those lists than on the random-number function. A generator cannot produce representation that its underlying data does not contain.","When a project needs a specific culture or historical period, use a curated source designed for it."]},
{heading:"Fictional characters",text:["For fiction, test a generated name against age, setting, family relationships, language, class, and genre. A plausible modern name may be wrong for a medieval setting or distractingly similar to another major character.","Say the names aloud together; visual and phonetic confusion can make scenes harder to follow."]},
{heading:"Games and NPCs",text:["Game masters can use random names to keep play moving when an unexpected NPC appears. A short list prepared by region or faction works better than one universal list because it reinforces worldbuilding while preserving speed.","Record accepted names immediately so the same NPC does not receive a different identity later."]},
{heading:"Test and demo data",text:["Generated names can make prototypes easier to read than rows called User 1 and User 2, but fictional names should never be mistaken for anonymization of real personal data. Replacing a real person’s name while leaving identifying attributes can still expose them.","For privacy-sensitive datasets, use an appropriate de-identification process rather than a name generator."]},
{heading:"Avoiding unintended real-person claims",text:["A random combination can match a real person. Do not attach fabricated crimes, medical facts, financial records, or other harmful claims to a generated name in a context that could be interpreted as factual.","Use clearly fictional context or synthetic identifiers when examples involve sensitive information."]},
{heading:"Uniqueness is not guaranteed",text:["Random selection does not guarantee that two outputs are different, especially when the source list is small. If an application requires unique identifiers, enforce uniqueness separately in the data model.","Names are display values; database keys should use a purpose-built identifier."]},
{heading:"Filtering and appropriateness",text:["Name lists can contain spellings, words, or combinations that are inappropriate in a particular language or audience. Human review remains important for public-facing products, classrooms, children’s content, and commercial releases.","A harmless component can become awkward when two randomly selected parts are combined."]},
{heading:"Shortlisting workflow",text:["Generate a group, remove obvious mismatches, then compare the remaining names against the role they need to serve. For creative work, changing one syllable, surname, or spelling can turn a near miss into a distinctive final choice.","Keep notes about why a candidate works instead of relying only on first impressions."]},
{heading:"Using this tool responsibly",text:["The generator is an ideation and placeholder utility. It does not determine ethnicity, nationality, gender identity, legal availability, trademark status, or whether a name is culturally appropriate. Those questions require separate context and, where relevant, research.","Use randomness for variety while keeping the final naming decision human."]},
]},
"tax-efficient-withdrawal-calculator":{title:"Tax-Efficient Withdrawal Calculator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"The withdrawal-order problem",text:["Retirement spending can come from taxable brokerage assets, tax-deferred accounts, Roth accounts, cash, and other sources. The order matters because withdrawals can create ordinary income, capital gains, taxes on Social Security, Medicare premium effects, and changes in future required distributions.","A tax-efficient sequence aims to manage lifetime after-tax resources, not merely minimize this year’s tax bill."]},
{heading:"Taxable accounts first is only a heuristic",text:["Spending taxable assets first can preserve tax-advantaged growth, but it is not universally optimal. Selling appreciated assets can realize capital gains, while deliberately taking some IRA income in a low bracket may prevent a much larger tax-deferred balance later.","Compare mixed withdrawals with simple account-by-account sequencing."]},
{heading:"Tax-deferred accounts",text:["Traditional IRA and pre-tax plan withdrawals are generally included in ordinary taxable income, subject to account rules and exceptions. Deferring every dollar can look attractive early in retirement but may create larger required minimum distributions and bracket pressure later.","Model the marginal tax rate on the next withdrawal rather than applying one flat rate to the entire account."]},
{heading:"Roth accounts",text:["Qualified Roth withdrawals generally do not add to federal taxable income, making Roth assets valuable for high-tax years, large purchases, or controlling adjusted gross income. That flexibility also gives Roth balances significant option value.","Using Roth first simply because the current tax is zero can sacrifice years of tax-free growth and future flexibility."]},
{heading:"Capital gains and basis",text:["A taxable-account sale is not automatically taxable on the full proceeds. Tax generally depends on realized gain: sale proceeds minus applicable cost basis, with different treatment possible for short- and long-term gains. Losses can also affect the result.","Track basis by holding when possible; a withdrawal amount and a taxable gain are different numbers."]},
{heading:"Social Security interaction",text:["Additional income can cause a larger portion of Social Security benefits to become taxable under federal rules. This creates effective marginal-rate zones where an extra dollar of IRA withdrawal can increase taxable income by more than one dollar.","A retirement tax model should consider benefit taxation instead of looking only at the nominal bracket."]},
{heading:"Medicare and income thresholds",text:["Modified adjusted gross income can affect Medicare income-related premium surcharges using a lookback period. Large conversions, realized gains, or withdrawals may therefore have costs beyond the income tax shown on a simple return.","Threshold effects make multi-year planning more useful than optimizing each year independently."]},
{heading:"RMDs and future years",text:["Required minimum distribution rules can force taxable withdrawals later in life. Earlier partial withdrawals or Roth conversions may reduce future RMD exposure, but the benefit depends on current versus future rates, investment growth, age, and estate goals.","Use the calculator as a scenario tool and verify current RMD ages and rules for the relevant year."]},
{heading:"A worked planning approach",text:["Estimate annual spending, subtract reliable cash income, then identify the remaining portfolio need. Fill intentionally chosen tax brackets with tax-deferred income, realize gains when appropriate, and use Roth dollars strategically for the remainder. Recalculate taxes after each major layer because interactions are not always linear.","The best sequence can change after a home sale, pension start, Social Security claim, inheritance, or major charitable gift."]},
{heading:"Limits of a simplified calculator",text:["State taxes, deductions, credits, qualified charitable distributions, inherited accounts, early-distribution rules, net investment income tax, account-specific basis, and estate planning can materially change an optimal sequence. A general calculator cannot encode every household rule.","Use its output to compare strategies, then validate a high-stakes withdrawal plan with current tax rules or a qualified professional."]},
]},
"payoff-date-calculator":{title:"Payoff Date Calculator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What determines a payoff date",text:["For an amortizing debt, payoff timing is driven by the current principal balance, periodic interest rate, required payment, payment frequency, and any additional principal. Interest is charged on the outstanding balance, so reducing principal earlier usually shortens the schedule and lowers future interest.","The calculator estimates a schedule from the assumptions entered; the lender’s official payoff quote controls an actual closing."]},
{heading:"Payment must exceed accruing interest",text:["A debt will not amortize normally if the payment is less than or equal to the interest accruing during the period. In that case the balance can remain flat or grow through negative amortization.","If a calculator cannot find a payoff date, first compare the periodic payment with periodic interest."]},
{heading:"How amortization changes over time",text:["With a fixed payment and fixed rate, early payments contain more interest because the balance is larger. As principal falls, interest falls and more of the same payment reaches principal. This is why a modest recurring extra payment can remove many late payments from a long loan.","Do not estimate payoff by simply dividing balance by payment when interest is material."]},
{heading:"Extra principal payments",text:["An extra payment marked for principal lowers the balance used for future interest calculations. A recurring extra amount generally has a larger cumulative effect than the same amount paid much later. Confirm that the servicer applies extra funds to principal rather than advancing the next due date.","Keep the normal required payment in the model even when adding principal."]},
{heading:"One-time lump sums",text:["Bonuses, tax refunds, asset sales, or other lump sums can be modeled at the date they will actually be paid. Their effect depends on timing because an earlier principal reduction prevents more future interest.","Compare the interest saved with the value and liquidity of the cash used for the lump sum."]},
{heading:"Interest-rate assumptions",text:["Fixed-rate debt is straightforward if the rate remains unchanged. Adjustable-rate loans, promotional rates, credit cards, and variable lines of credit require scenarios because future rates can change the payoff date.","A single date for variable-rate debt is conditional on the assumed rate path."]},
{heading:"Fees and payoff quotes",text:["A lender payoff amount can differ from the statement balance because interest accrues between the statement date and payoff date and because contractual fees or credits may apply. Mortgages can also involve escrow balances that are accounted for separately.","Use the calculator for planning; request an official dated payoff statement for a transaction."]},
{heading:"Comparing acceleration strategies",text:["Compare the baseline schedule with a fixed extra monthly payment, a one-time principal payment, and any biweekly strategy you actually intend to use. Evaluate both months saved and total interest saved.","A strategy is sustainable only if the extra payment fits the household cash-flow plan."]},
{heading:"Debt priority",text:["The fastest payoff of one account is not always the best portfolio-wide debt strategy. When several debts exist, interest rates, minimum payments, tax treatment, penalties, and behavioral preferences can affect which balance should receive extra cash first.","Use account-level payoff dates alongside a complete debt list."]},
{heading:"Reading the result",text:["Treat the estimated payoff month as a consequence of the entered payment pattern. Recalculate after rate changes, skipped payments, refinances, new charges, or large principal reductions. Small discrepancies can also arise from lender-specific daily interest and rounding conventions.","The most useful output is the change in payoff time and interest between realistic alternatives, not false precision about one future date."]},
]},
"down-payment-calculator":{title:"Down Payment Calculator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"Down payment basics",text:["A down payment is the portion of a purchase price paid upfront rather than financed. For a home, the dollar amount and percentage affect the mortgage principal, loan-to-value ratio, cash needed at closing, and sometimes mortgage-insurance requirements or pricing.","Down payment is only one part of cash-to-close; closing costs and reserves may be separate."]},
{heading:"From percentage to dollars",text:["Multiply purchase price by the down-payment percentage to estimate the upfront contribution. A $400,000 home with 10% down implies $40,000 before other closing items, while the starting loan amount is roughly $360,000 if no financed fees are added.","Always distinguish the percentage of purchase price from the percentage of the loan."]},
{heading:"Loan-to-value ratio",text:["LTV compares the loan balance with the property value used by the lender. A larger down payment generally produces a lower LTV, which can affect eligibility, mortgage insurance, interest-rate pricing, and refinance flexibility.","Appraised value can matter if it differs from the contract price."]},
{heading:"Mortgage insurance",text:["Conventional loans with smaller down payments may require private mortgage insurance, while government-backed programs use their own mortgage-insurance or guarantee-fee structures. The cost and cancellation rules differ by program.","Do not assume that reaching a particular down-payment percentage has identical effects across all loan types."]},
{heading:"Closing costs are separate",text:["Lender fees, title or settlement charges, taxes, prepaid interest, insurance, and escrow funding can require additional cash. Seller credits or lender credits may offset some items subject to program rules.","Budgeting only the down payment can leave a buyer short at closing."]},
{heading:"Emergency reserves",text:["Putting every available dollar into the property can reduce the mortgage but also leave little cash for repairs, moving, furnishing, job disruption, or deductibles. Some lenders also require documented reserves for particular borrowers or property types.","Compare a larger down payment with retaining a deliberate post-closing cash buffer."]},
{heading:"Monthly-payment tradeoff",text:["More money down lowers the financed principal and therefore usually lowers principal-and-interest payment. It may also change insurance or pricing. But the cash used upfront no longer remains liquid or available for another goal.","Evaluate both monthly affordability and the balance sheet after closing."]},
{heading:"Saving timeline",text:["If the calculator is used as a savings target, divide the remaining down-payment goal by realistic monthly savings and include expected interest only when it is appropriate for the account. Home-price changes can move the target while you save.","Track the goal in dollars as well as percentage because the purchase price may change."]},
{heading:"Gift funds and assistance",text:["Some loan programs allow eligible gift funds, grants, or down-payment assistance subject to documentation and program rules. Assistance can have income limits, occupancy requirements, second liens, repayment conditions, or resale restrictions.","Verify the exact program rather than treating assistance as unrestricted cash."]},
{heading:"Choosing a target",text:["There is no universal best percentage. Compare scenarios such as minimum eligible down payment, an intermediate amount, and a larger amount that changes mortgage-insurance or pricing economics. Include cash-to-close and reserves in every scenario.","The best target balances loan cost, approval constraints, monthly payment, liquidity, and the buyer’s other priorities."]},
]},
"sleep-debt-calculator":{title:"Sleep Debt Calculator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What sleep debt means",text:["Sleep debt is a simple way to describe the gap between a person’s sleep need and the sleep they actually obtain over a period. If someone needs eight hours but sleeps six and a half, the arithmetic shortfall is 1.5 hours for that night.","The number is a planning estimate, not a clinical measurement of accumulated biological damage."]},
{heading:"Choosing a sleep-need target",text:["Adults vary in sleep need, and recommended ranges depend on age. A useful target should reflect age guidance plus personal functioning rather than an arbitrary ideal copied from someone else.","If you consistently feel restored and function well at a stable schedule, that history is more informative than chasing minute-level precision."]},
{heading:"Night-by-night calculation",text:["Calculate each day’s deficit as target sleep minus actual sleep, counting zero when actual sleep meets or exceeds the target unless the tool explicitly tracks surplus. Summing several nights shows how much scheduled sleep was missed relative to the target.","A weekly total can hide one severely short night, so review the daily pattern too."]},
{heading:"Why one long sleep is not a perfect reset",text:["Sleeping longer after restriction can improve alertness, but recovery from repeated short sleep is not always equivalent to repaying a bank balance hour for hour. Performance, mood, appetite, and circadian timing may recover on different schedules.","Use debt as a signal to restore consistent opportunity for sleep rather than as permission for repeated restriction."]},
{heading:"Circadian timing",text:["Sleep duration and sleep timing interact. Eight hours scheduled at a biologically difficult time may not feel equivalent to eight hours aligned with a stable circadian schedule, especially for shift workers or people crossing time zones.","A duration calculator does not measure circadian misalignment."]},
{heading:"Naps",text:["Naps can improve alertness and add sleep time, but late or long naps can also reduce sleep pressure and make nighttime sleep harder for some people. Whether to count a nap depends on what the calculator defines as total daily sleep.","Keep the counting rule consistent across the week."]},
{heading:"Weekend catch-up",text:["Large weekend sleep-ins may reflect substantial weekday restriction. Catch-up sleep can be helpful, but a repeating cycle of short weekdays and very late weekends can also shift timing and make Monday sleep more difficult.","Compare average duration with schedule regularity, not only the weekly total."]},
{heading:"Tracking actual sleep",text:["Time in bed is not identical to sleep duration. Wearables estimate sleep with imperfect algorithms, while manual logs depend on recall. For personal trend tracking, consistency of measurement often matters more than treating any device estimate as exact.","Use the same method when comparing one week with another."]},
{heading:"When the number is concerning",text:["Persistent excessive sleepiness, loud snoring with breathing pauses, insomnia, unplanned dozing, or difficulty staying awake while driving deserves more attention than a calculator score. Sleep disorders and medical conditions cannot be diagnosed from a debt total.","Avoid driving or hazardous work when dangerously sleepy and seek appropriate medical evaluation for persistent symptoms."]},
{heading:"Using the result",text:["Translate the estimate into schedule changes: protect a realistic bedtime, reduce avoidable late-night commitments, plan recovery after unusual short sleep, and reassess after several consistent nights.","The goal is sustainable sleep opportunity and daytime function, not merely making the displayed debt equal zero."]},
]},
"weekly-budget-calculator":{title:"Weekly Budget Calculator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"Why budget by week",text:["A weekly budget turns monthly or annual income and expenses into a shorter feedback cycle. It is useful for groceries, transport, dining, entertainment, and other categories where spending decisions happen frequently.","Weekly budgeting does not change the economics; it changes how quickly you notice drift."]},
{heading:"Convert income consistently",text:["If income is monthly or annual, convert it using a consistent method. Dividing annual income by 52 gives an average week, while simply dividing a monthly number by four overstates the weekly amount because a year has about 4.33 weeks per month.","Use take-home income when the budget is intended to manage spendable cash."]},
{heading:"Fixed versus variable expenses",text:["Rent, loan payments, subscriptions, and insurance are relatively fixed; groceries, fuel, entertainment, and discretionary shopping vary. Converting fixed monthly bills to weekly equivalents helps reserve money before variable spending consumes it.","A weekly allowance should not make next month’s rent look like unused cash."]},
{heading:"Irregular expenses",text:["Annual premiums, gifts, repairs, school costs, travel, and other non-monthly expenses need sinking funds. Estimate the annual amount and reserve a weekly share so the eventual bill is funded gradually.","Ignoring irregular costs makes an ordinary month look artificially affordable."]},
{heading:"Savings as a planned category",text:["Treat emergency savings, retirement contributions, debt acceleration, or other goals as deliberate allocations rather than whatever remains by accident. The calculator’s annual-savings projection is only realistic if the weekly amount is actually transferred or retained.","Automating the transfer can turn a budget assumption into behavior."]},
{heading:"Weekly spending limit",text:["After reserving fixed obligations, irregular costs, and savings, the remaining amount can be divided among flexible categories. A single total is simple, while category caps give more diagnostic detail.","Choose enough categories to guide decisions without making tracking burdensome."]},
{heading:"Five-week calendar months",text:["Some months contain five occurrences of a particular weekday or five weekly shopping cycles. A strict four-weeks-per-month plan can therefore fail even when each week looks normal.","Annualizing by 52 weeks avoids much of this calendar distortion."]},
{heading:"Variable income",text:["Freelancers and hourly workers may not have a stable weekly paycheck. Build the core budget around a conservative baseline, keep a buffer for lean weeks, and separate tax reserves when income is not fully withheld.","Do not commit recurring expenses based solely on an unusually strong week."]},
{heading:"Reviewing variance",text:["Compare planned and actual spending each week, but investigate the cause rather than treating every overage as failure. A grocery spike from stocking staples differs from a recurring pattern of overspending.","Roll-forward rules should be explicit: decide whether unused category money carries over or moves to savings."]},
{heading:"Reading the calculator outputs",text:["Savings rate, weekly spending, and annual savings describe different aspects of the plan. A high savings rate can still coexist with poor liquidity if irregular bills were omitted, while a balanced weekly plan can fail if income was entered before taxes instead of after taxes.","Use the output as a cash-flow plan and reconcile it with real account activity."]},
]},
"binary-text-converter":{title:"Binary Text Converter Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What the converter actually does",text:["A binary-text converter maps text characters to bytes using a character encoding and displays those byte values as binary, or performs the reverse operation. Binary is only a representation of numbers; the crucial bridge between numbers and characters is the encoding.","Without knowing the encoding, the same byte sequence cannot always be interpreted as the intended text."]},
{heading:"Bytes and bits",text:["A byte normally contains eight bits, so the decimal byte value 65 is written as 01000001 in eight-bit binary. Text encoded as UTF-8 becomes a sequence of bytes, and each byte can be rendered as eight binary digits.","Preserving leading zeroes makes byte boundaries visible and avoids ambiguity."]},
{heading:"ASCII basics",text:["ASCII defines codes for basic English letters, digits, punctuation, and control characters. Uppercase A is decimal 65, while lowercase a is 97. ASCII fits within one byte and is also the first part of UTF-8.","ASCII examples are useful for learning, but modern text cannot be modeled as ASCII only."]},
{heading:"UTF-8 and Unicode",text:["Unicode assigns code points to characters across writing systems and symbols. UTF-8 encodes those code points into one to four bytes. An emoji or many non-Latin characters therefore produce several binary byte groups rather than one eight-bit value.","Do not assume one visible character always equals one byte."]},
{heading:"Text to binary example",text:["For ASCII text Hi, H is decimal 72 or 01001000 and i is decimal 105 or 01101001. The binary output can be shown as two groups: 01001000 01101001. A UTF-8 encoder follows the same byte-display idea for broader characters.","Spaces between groups are formatting aids and are not additional data bytes."]},
{heading:"Binary back to text",text:["Decoding requires splitting the input into valid byte values, converting each group to a byte, and decoding the byte sequence with the expected encoding. Missing bits, non-binary characters, or invalid UTF-8 sequences can make decoding fail or produce replacement characters.","Validate group length and character set before assuming a strange result is meaningful text."]},
{heading:"Whitespace and separators",text:["Some tools accept spaces between bytes while others expect a continuous bit string. Newlines, tabs, and spaces can themselves be encoded text characters, so distinguish formatting separators from data that should be decoded.","When sharing binary output, document the grouping convention."]},
{heading:"Binary is not encryption",text:["Converting readable text to zeroes and ones does not make it secret. Anyone can reverse the representation when the encoding is known or inferred. Base64, hexadecimal, and binary are encodings or representations, not cryptographic protection.","Use established encryption when confidentiality is the requirement."]},
{heading:"Files versus text",text:["Arbitrary files are also byte sequences, but interpreting those bytes as text can corrupt data or produce invalid characters. A text converter is designed for character data, not as a general-purpose binary file editor.","Use file-aware tools when exact byte preservation matters."]},
{heading:"Practical uses",text:["Binary conversion is useful for teaching encodings, debugging protocols, inspecting simple byte sequences, CTF exercises, and understanding how characters are stored. For software debugging, hexadecimal is often more compact, but binary makes individual bits easy to see.","The important lesson is the chain: character → encoding → bytes → numeric representation."]},
]},
"bone-density-risk-calculator":{title:"Bone Density Risk Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What this calculator scores",text:["This tool assigns points for age, sex, low BMI, smoking duration, alcohol intake, family history, corticosteroid use and calcium intake. It converts the point total into a percentage of its own maximum score and labels the result low, moderate or high risk.","That percentage is a model score, not a measured bone-density percentage and not a validated 10-year fracture probability. The calculator does not replace DXA testing or FRAX."]},
{heading:"Bone density versus fracture risk",text:["Bone mineral density is commonly measured by dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA), often at the hip and spine. Fracture risk depends on bone density plus age, prior fractures, falls, medications and other clinical factors. Two people with the same BMD can therefore have different fracture risk.","This calculator focuses on risk-factor screening because it does not ask for a measured femoral-neck BMD or T-score."]},
{heading:"Age and sex",text:["Bone loss and fracture risk generally increase with age. Men and women can both develop osteoporosis, although postmenopausal bone loss makes screening particularly important for many women. The tool adds points for age bands and for female sex as a broad population-level signal.","Those points are not individualized enough to decide whether someone needs medication. Screening recommendations and treatment decisions use age, fracture history and clinical context more precisely."]},
{heading:"Low body weight",text:["Low BMI is associated with lower bone mass and can increase fracture vulnerability. The calculator adds more points below BMI 18.5 and a smaller amount below BMI 21. But BMI cannot reveal muscle mass, nutritional status or the cause of low weight.","Unintentional weight loss, eating disorders or malabsorption deserve attention beyond simply raising calcium intake because the underlying condition may affect bone health."]},
{heading:"Smoking and alcohol",text:["Smoking is associated with poorer bone health and fracture risk, and the tool uses years of smoking as a simple exposure measure. Alcohol is scored by drinks per week. These inputs are coarse: intensity, past versus current smoking, binge patterns and lifetime exposure are not modeled.","Reducing smoking and excessive alcohol exposure can support overall health, but changing these fields in the calculator should not be interpreted as an immediate reversal of established osteoporosis."]},
{heading:"Family history and prior fracture",text:["A family history of osteoporosis raises the calculator score. In real fracture assessment, a parental hip fracture can be particularly informative. Personal history of a fragility fracture is also clinically important, but the current calculator does not collect it.","Because that major factor is absent, someone with a prior low-trauma fracture should not rely on a low online score for reassurance."]},
{heading:"Corticosteroid exposure",text:["Long-term systemic glucocorticoid therapy can accelerate bone loss and increase fracture risk. The calculator treats corticosteroid use as a yes/no risk factor, but actual risk depends on dose, duration and route. Short topical use is not equivalent to prolonged systemic treatment.","People using chronic systemic steroids should discuss bone protection and appropriate assessment with their clinician rather than using the score as the deciding threshold."]},
{heading:"Calcium and vitamin D",text:["The calculator scores low calcium intake and displays a calcium target. Calcium is necessary for skeletal health, but more is not automatically better and diet, supplements, kidney function and other medical factors matter. Vitamin D supports calcium absorption, yet the tool does not measure vitamin-D status.","The displayed supplement suggestions are generic model text, not a personalized prescription. Dietary intake and clinician guidance should determine whether supplementation is appropriate."]},
{heading:"DXA, T-scores and Z-scores",text:["DXA results commonly report a T-score comparing bone density with a young-adult reference and a Z-score comparing with an age/sex reference. In appropriate populations, a T-score at or below -2.5 is commonly used in the diagnostic framework for osteoporosis, while values between -1.0 and -2.5 indicate low bone mass.","This risk calculator has no T-score input, so it cannot infer those categories. Its recommendation to consider DXA is a screening prompt only."]},
{heading:"Falls and strength",text:["Fractures occur when bone strength and mechanical load intersect. Fall risk, balance, vision, sedating medications, muscle weakness and the home environment can therefore matter even when BMD is only moderately reduced. None of these are captured by the current point score.","Weight-bearing activity and resistance training can support bone and muscle health, but exercise should be adapted to ability and existing fracture risk."]},
{heading:"How to interpret the result",text:["Use the score to inventory modifiable and non-modifiable risk factors. A high score supports discussing formal assessment; a low score does not rule out osteoporosis, particularly when important unmodeled factors such as prior fragility fracture or early menopause are present.","Do not compare the displayed risk percentage with FRAX percentages. They are different calculations with different meanings."]},]},
"night-shift-health-calculator":{title:"Night Shift Health Impact Calculator",sections:[{heading:"What the score summarizes",text:["This calculator combines years of night work, night shifts per week, sleep obtained after a shift, caffeine intake, exercise frequency, meal timing and light-management habits into a health-impact score. It also displays circadian-disruption, cardiovascular, type-2-diabetes and cancer-related messages. These are risk-context outputs, not individualized disease probabilities.","Night work affects people differently because schedule direction, shift length, chronotype, commute, family responsibilities and rotation pattern matter. The current inputs capture several major behavioral factors but cannot model every occupational schedule. Treat the result as a structured way to identify modifiable stressors."]},{heading:"Circadian disruption",text:["Human circadian rhythms coordinate sleep propensity, alertness, hormone timing, body temperature and metabolism across roughly 24 hours. Working during the biological night and sleeping during daylight can place behavior out of phase with these rhythms. Rapidly rotating schedules can create repeated transitions rather than allowing stable adaptation.","The calculator uses shift frequency and light management as proxies for this disruption. It cannot measure melatonin timing or an individual's circadian phase, so the circadian label should be interpreted qualitatively rather than as a laboratory measurement."]},{heading:"Sleep after a night shift",text:["Sleep duration after the shift is one of the most direct inputs because daytime sleep is often shorter and more fragmented than nighttime sleep. Light, noise, household activity and circadian wake signals can all shorten the sleep episode. Repeated six-hour post-shift sleeps can accumulate meaningful sleep restriction even when a worker feels adapted to the schedule.","Protecting a dark, quiet and cool sleep environment, reducing interruptions and planning a consistent sleep window can improve opportunity. Severe sleepiness, near-misses while driving home, loud snoring or breathing pauses are reasons for clinical evaluation rather than simply increasing the calculator's sleep value."]},{heading:"Light management",text:["Light is the strongest environmental signal for the circadian clock. Bright light during the work period can support alertness, while reducing bright morning light on the commute and keeping the bedroom dark may help some workers protect daytime sleep. The appropriate strategy depends on whether the goal is partial adaptation, full adaptation or rapid return to daytime life.","The calculator simplifies this into managed versus unmanaged light exposure. That cannot represent timing, intensity or wavelength, so use the field as a reminder that light timing is part of shift-work planning, not as a prescription for a specific light-therapy protocol."]},{heading:"Caffeine timing",text:["Caffeine can improve alertness during a night shift, but dose and timing matter. Because caffeine can remain active for hours, large amounts late in the shift may make daytime sleep harder even if the worker feels tired. The calculator accepts milligrams per day, which is useful for seeing total exposure but does not know when each dose was consumed.","A practical strategy is to use the minimum amount needed for alertness and avoid relying on repeated late-shift doses that collide with the planned sleep period. Individual sensitivity, pregnancy, medications and cardiovascular conditions can change what intake is appropriate."]},{heading:"Meal timing and metabolism",text:["Night work can shift food intake into the biological night, when glucose handling and digestive physiology differ from daytime patterns. Irregular meals, large overnight meals and limited healthy food availability can compound the challenge. The calculator's regular-versus-irregular meal input is a simple representation of this issue.","Regular does not mean that one universal meal schedule is optimal. Workers should consider total diet quality, portion size, gastrointestinal tolerance and the timing of the main sleep episode. The tool cannot estimate blood glucose or diagnose metabolic disease."]},{heading:"Exercise and recovery",text:["Regular physical activity supports cardiovascular and metabolic health and can be especially valuable for workers with sedentary jobs. The exercise-days input captures frequency, not intensity or duration. Three short walks and three hard training sessions are therefore treated similarly even though their training load differs.","Exercise timing should also respect sleep. A workout that consistently delays the post-shift sleep window may be counterproductive for a severely sleep-restricted worker. Use the field to identify inactivity, then build a sustainable routine around the actual roster."]},{heading:"Long-term risk messages",text:["The page displays cardiovascular, type-2-diabetes and cancer-related risk context because long-term shift work has been studied in relation to these outcomes. Observational associations do not mean that a particular worker will develop disease, and risk depends on many factors outside the calculator including smoking, body composition, blood pressure, family history and socioeconomic conditions.","The cancer classification message should not be interpreted as a personal cancer diagnosis or probability. Occupational epidemiology describes population-level evidence. Individual screening decisions should follow age, history, symptoms and professional guidance."]},{heading:"Worked schedule comparison",text:["Imagine two workers with the same five years of night-shift history and three shifts per week. One sleeps 5.5 hours after shifts, uses large late caffeine doses, has irregular meals and unmanaged morning light. The other protects 7.5 hours of sleep, manages light, exercises regularly and keeps meals more consistent. The calculator is designed to show why identical job tenure can produce different modifiable-risk profiles.","That comparison is more useful than obsessing over a one-point score change. Change one input at a time to identify which habits the model treats as most influential, then choose interventions that are feasible within the worker's roster and home responsibilities."]},{heading:"Safety and limitations",text:["Night-shift fatigue can create immediate safety risks before long-term health effects become relevant. If a worker is struggling to stay awake during a commute or safety-critical task, a low online health-risk score does not make the situation safe. Fatigue management, rest and workplace procedures take priority.","The calculator cannot diagnose shift-work sleep disorder, insomnia, sleep apnea, diabetes, cardiovascular disease or cancer. Persistent insomnia or excessive sleepiness associated with the work schedule deserves appropriate clinical assessment. Use the tool for education and habit review, not medical clearance for night work."]},]},
"oral-health-risk-calculator":{title:"Oral Health Risk Calculator",sections:[{heading:"What the score represents",text:["This calculator combines brushing frequency, flossing frequency, daily sugar exposure, smoking, alcohol intake, time since the last dental visit, dry mouth, nighttime grinding and acidic-food exposure into an educational oral-risk score. The score is a screening-style summary of entered behaviors and risk factors; it is not a diagnosis of cavities, periodontitis, oral cancer or tooth wear.","Oral diseases have different causes, so one combined number should always be unpacked. Caries risk is strongly influenced by repeated fermentable-carbohydrate exposure, fluoride, saliva and plaque control. Gum disease is influenced by plaque, smoking and host factors. Oral-cancer risk has a different profile, particularly tobacco and alcohol exposure. The page therefore also reports a separate cancer-risk message."]},{heading:"Brushing and fluoride",text:["Brushing frequency is one of the most actionable inputs. Brushing twice daily with fluoride toothpaste helps disrupt plaque and repeatedly exposes teeth to fluoride, which supports remineralization and makes enamel more resistant to acid attack. Technique and duration matter too; two rushed passes are not equivalent to thorough cleaning along the gumline and all tooth surfaces.","The calculator records frequency but cannot see fluoride concentration, brushing technique, an electric versus manual brush, gum recession, orthodontic appliances or existing restorations. If risk remains high despite frequent brushing, do not assume more brushing force is the answer. Aggressive brushing can contribute to abrasion and recession."]},{heading:"Flossing and interdental cleaning",text:["Flossing frequency approximates interdental plaque control, where a toothbrush may not clean effectively. Daily interdental cleaning is especially relevant for gingival inflammation and areas where teeth contact closely. Some people use interdental brushes, water flossers or other devices instead of string floss; the calculator's flossing field should be interpreted as a proxy for effective between-teeth cleaning.","Bleeding during flossing can occur with inflamed gums and should not automatically be interpreted as a reason to stop cleaning. Persistent bleeding, swelling, recession, loose teeth or bad breath warrants dental assessment because a calculator cannot measure periodontal pocket depth or bone loss."]},{heading:"Sugar frequency and dental caries",text:["The sugar input is servings per day, but frequency of exposure often matters as much as the total amount. Oral bacteria metabolize fermentable carbohydrates and produce acids that lower plaque pH. Repeated snacks or sweet drinks can create repeated acid challenges with less recovery time for saliva-driven remineralization.","A single dessert with a meal may create a different exposure pattern from sipping a sweet beverage for hours even if grams of sugar are similar. The tool cannot model that timing. For scenario testing, reducing the number of separate sugary exposures is often more informative than merely changing an abstract serving size."]},{heading:"Acidic foods, drinks and erosion",text:["The acidic-diet scale addresses erosive exposure, which differs from bacterial tooth decay. Soft drinks, energy drinks, citrus products and other acidic items can directly soften enamel. Frequent exposure, swishing, or brushing immediately after an acid challenge may increase wear in susceptible people.","Acid erosion also depends on saliva, reflux, vomiting, beverage pH and contact time. If enamel is thinning, teeth are becoming sensitive, or edges appear translucent, a professional examination is more useful than trying to infer severity from the calculator's 0-to-5 diet rating."]},{heading:"Dry mouth",text:["Dry mouth, or xerostomia, matters because saliva buffers acids, clears food, supports remineralization and helps control the oral environment. Medications, dehydration, autoimmune disease, radiation therapy and other conditions can reduce salivary flow. People with persistent dry mouth can develop cavities rapidly even with habits that previously seemed adequate.","The calculator uses dry mouth as a yes/no factor and cannot measure actual salivary flow. Persistent symptoms, difficulty swallowing dry foods, frequent nighttime thirst or a sudden increase in decay should prompt discussion with a dentist or clinician, including review of medications and possible saliva-support strategies."]},{heading:"Smoking, alcohol and cancer risk",text:["Tobacco exposure is a major oral-health concern because it increases periodontal risk and is strongly associated with cancers of the oral cavity and related structures. Alcohol can compound oral-cancer risk, particularly when combined with tobacco. The calculator includes current smoking and weekly alcohol but does not capture lifetime exposure, smokeless tobacco, vaping details or prior cessation.","A low calculated score cannot rule out oral cancer. A mouth ulcer, red or white patch, unexplained lump, persistent hoarseness, difficulty swallowing or lesion that does not heal deserves professional evaluation regardless of the score. Screening value comes from recognizing risk and symptoms, not from obtaining a reassuring number."]},{heading:"Dental visits and prevention",text:["Months since the last dental visit is a proxy for professional prevention and examination. Appropriate recall intervals differ by risk; someone with active periodontal disease, high caries risk or complex restorations may need more frequent care than a person with stable oral health. The calculator should not be used to choose a universal interval.","Dental visits can identify problems that are invisible to a self-report tool, including early decay between teeth, periodontal pockets, cracked restorations and suspicious soft-tissue lesions. Use the field to notice prolonged gaps in care, then discuss an individualized recall schedule with a dental professional."]},{heading:"Bruxism and tooth wear",text:["Night grinding, or bruxism, can contribute to tooth wear, fractures, muscle soreness and jaw symptoms. It is not primarily a plaque or cavity mechanism, which is why its interpretation differs from brushing and sugar inputs. Stress, sleep factors and medications can be relevant, but causes vary.","The calculator can flag grinding but cannot determine whether a night guard is appropriate, whether wear is active, or whether jaw pain comes from bruxism. A fitted appliance protects teeth in selected cases but does not automatically eliminate the underlying behavior."]},{heading:"How to use the result",text:["Use the result to identify which modifiable inputs are pushing risk upward. Change one behavior at a time in the calculator—for example, brushing from once to twice daily, reducing sugar exposures, or shortening an overdue dental interval—to see how the model responds. That creates a practical prevention checklist rather than treating the score as a prediction.","The strongest use of this tool is educational triage. It cannot inspect teeth, gums or oral tissues, take radiographs, measure pockets or diagnose disease. Pain, swelling, facial infection, trauma, uncontrolled bleeding or suspicious lesions require appropriate professional care regardless of the numerical category."]},]},
"closing-cost-calculator":{title:"Closing Cost Calculator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What closing costs include",text:["Closing costs are transaction expenses and prepaid items paid in addition to the property price. They can include lender charges, appraisal, title or settlement services, recording fees, taxes, prepaid interest, insurance, and initial escrow deposits.","The exact list varies by loan, property, jurisdiction, and closing date."]},
{heading:"Loan amount versus purchase price",text:["Some charges are based on the loan amount while others depend on purchase price, service provider, or a flat fee. A percentage estimate is convenient for early planning but should not be mistaken for an itemized quote.","Use the calculator’s loan amount and fee fields consistently so percentage-based costs are not double counted."]},
{heading:"Lender fees",text:["Origination, underwriting, processing, application, and discount-point charges may appear in the financing package. Points are generally expressed as a percentage of the loan amount and can be used to change the interest rate.","Compare rate-and-fee combinations over the expected holding period rather than choosing the lowest advertised rate automatically."]},
{heading:"Third-party services",text:["Appraisal, credit, flood determination, title search, title insurance, settlement, attorney, survey, and inspection costs can involve independent providers. Which services can be shopped depends on the transaction and loan process.","A calculator estimate should leave room for local quotes instead of inventing universal fees."]},
{heading:"Prepaid items",text:["Prepaid interest, homeowner’s insurance, property taxes, and escrow deposits are often collected at closing but are economically different from lender fees. Some are advance funding of expenses the owner would pay anyway.","Separating prepaids from transaction fees makes comparisons between lenders clearer."]},
{heading:"Seller and lender credits",text:["A seller may contribute toward eligible closing costs subject to contract and loan-program limits. A lender credit can also offset upfront costs, usually in exchange for different pricing such as a higher rate.","A credit changes who pays at closing; it does not necessarily make the underlying cost disappear."]},
{heading:"Cash to close",text:["Cash to close combines the down payment with closing costs and other adjustments, then subtracts deposits, credits, and financing where allowed. It is therefore not the same number as closing costs alone.","Budget the full cash-to-close requirement and maintain reserves after the transaction."]},
{heading:"Refinance closing costs",text:["Refinancing can involve many of the same lender and title charges but no purchase down payment. Costs may be paid in cash, offset by lender credit, or sometimes added to the new balance.","A no-cash-closing refinance can still have a meaningful economic cost."]},
{heading:"Break-even analysis",text:["When paying points or other optional upfront costs for a lower rate, compare the extra upfront amount with expected monthly savings. The simple break-even month is extra cost divided by monthly savings, though taxes and time value can refine the comparison.","If you expect to sell or refinance before break-even, the upfront choice may not pay back."]},
{heading:"From estimate to final numbers",text:["Early calculators are for planning. As the transaction progresses, use the lender’s Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure, title or settlement statements, and local tax information to replace assumptions with actual figures.","Investigate large changes between estimates and final disclosures before signing."]},
]},
"food-sensitivity-calculator":{title:"Food Sensitivity Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What this score actually represents",text:["This tool combines self-rated reactions to gluten, dairy, eggs, soy and nuts with the number of selected symptoms and an overall reaction-severity rating. The implemented formula creates a 0–100 burden score; it is an educational symptom-tracking score, not a validated diagnostic test for food allergy, intolerance, celiac disease or another gastrointestinal condition.","The highest-rated food is displayed as the top sensitivity, but that label means only that it received the largest user-entered score. It does not establish causation."]},
{heading:"Sensitivity, intolerance and allergy are different",text:["People often use 'food sensitivity' as a broad term, but medically important mechanisms differ. IgE-mediated food allergy can cause hives, swelling, wheeze, vomiting or anaphylaxis and requires appropriate medical evaluation. Lactose intolerance reflects difficulty digesting lactose. Celiac disease is an immune-mediated condition triggered by gluten and requires a different diagnostic pathway.","Because these conditions are not interchangeable, a high burden score should lead to better history-taking rather than indiscriminate food avoidance or self-diagnosis."]},
{heading:"How the burden formula behaves",text:["The five food ratings are averaged and multiplied into the score, while symptom count is multiplied by the selected overall reaction severity. This means selecting many symptoms at high severity can move the burden score substantially even when individual food ratings are modest.","That structure is useful for organizing a symptom diary, but it cannot tell whether the symptoms share one cause. Fatigue, headache, bloating and skin symptoms can arise from many unrelated conditions."]},
{heading:"Food and symptom diary",text:["Before eliminating multiple foods, record what was eaten, portion size, timing and symptoms for a consistent period. Note whether symptoms occur minutes, hours or a day later and whether the same food is tolerated on other occasions. Reproducibility is more informative than a single memorable episode.","Also record confounders such as illness, sleep, menstrual cycle where relevant, alcohol, exercise and medications. A structured diary can reveal that symptoms follow meal size, lactose dose or another pattern rather than the food category initially suspected."]},
{heading:"Elimination and reintroduction",text:["A supervised elimination approach removes a plausible trigger for a defined period and then reintroduces it systematically to see whether symptoms recur. Removing five major food groups at once may make the diet difficult to interpret because improvement cannot be attributed to one food and nutritional adequacy becomes harder to maintain.","The calculator counts eliminated foods and flags nutrition risk when several are removed. That warning is important: long-term restriction should have a clear reason and a plan for adequate protein, energy, calcium, vitamins and other nutrients."]},
{heading:"Gluten deserves special caution",text:["If celiac disease is a possibility, starting a strict gluten-free diet before appropriate testing can complicate diagnosis because antibody and intestinal findings may change after gluten withdrawal. A symptom score cannot distinguish celiac disease from wheat allergy, non-celiac symptoms or unrelated digestive complaints.","Anyone considering major long-term gluten avoidance because of persistent symptoms should discuss appropriate evaluation before making the diagnostic picture harder to interpret."]},
{heading:"Dairy and lactose",text:["Dairy symptoms are not automatically milk-protein allergy. Lactose intolerance often produces dose-related bloating, gas or diarrhea because lactose is incompletely digested. Some people tolerate yogurt, hard cheese or smaller lactose portions differently from milk.","A dairy score therefore needs context: which product, what quantity and what symptom? The calculator intentionally cannot resolve those distinctions from a single 0–5 rating."]},
{heading:"Testing limitations",text:["The current calculator may display a testing recommendation at higher burden levels, but the numerical score itself does not validate commercial food-sensitivity panels. Tests marketed around food-specific IgG should not be treated as proof that a food causes symptoms simply because an antibody is detected.","Clinical evaluation is especially important when symptoms suggest allergy, celiac disease, inflammatory disease, significant weight loss, anemia or another condition where a restrictive diet could delay diagnosis."]},
{heading:"When symptoms are urgent",text:["Difficulty breathing, throat tightness, faintness, rapidly spreading hives or severe swelling after eating can represent anaphylaxis and requires emergency care. Blood in stool, persistent vomiting, dehydration, unexplained weight loss or severe abdominal pain also deserves medical assessment rather than repeated calculator scoring.","A low burden score does not make a severe single reaction safe. Severity and mechanism matter more than the average score in urgent situations."]},
{heading:"How to use the result",text:["Use the score as a starting snapshot. Identify the highest-rated food, the selected symptoms and how many foods are already excluded. Then improve the quality of the underlying observations with a diary or professionally guided challenge rather than trying to drive the score down by eliminating more foods.","If you repeat the calculator, keep the scoring method consistent. A lower score is meaningful only if the same definitions and observation period were used."]},]},
"obesity-comorbidity-calculator":{title:"Obesity Comorbidity Risk Guide",sections:[
{heading:"What this score can and cannot mean",text:["An obesity-comorbidity calculator combines body-size and health-history inputs into a risk-oriented summary. It can highlight factors commonly associated with cardiometabolic disease, but its displayed score is not automatically a validated probability of diabetes, heart attack, or another diagnosis.","Interpret the output according to the calculator’s defined scoring method, not as a clinical prediction unless it has been validated for that purpose."]},
{heading:"BMI as one input",text:["BMI relates weight to height and is useful for population screening, but it does not directly measure body fat distribution, muscle mass, or metabolic health. Athletes, older adults, and people with different body compositions can have the same BMI with different risk profiles.","Use BMI alongside other information rather than as a complete health assessment."]},
{heading:"Waist and fat distribution",text:["Central adiposity is particularly relevant to metabolic risk. Waist circumference or waist-to-height measures can add information that BMI misses because visceral fat is associated with insulin resistance and cardiovascular risk.","Measurement technique matters; compare measurements taken at the same anatomical location."]},
{heading:"Blood pressure",text:["Hypertension commonly clusters with obesity and increases cardiovascular and kidney risk. One elevated reading does not establish a diagnosis because technique, stress, caffeine, exercise, and medication can affect the result.","Use properly measured repeated readings and clinical guidance when blood pressure is persistently high."]},
{heading:"Glucose and diabetes",text:["Insulin resistance, prediabetes, and type 2 diabetes are important obesity-related comorbidities. A questionnaire or score cannot substitute for laboratory measures such as fasting glucose or HbA1c when testing is indicated.","Known diabetes changes risk management regardless of what a simple calculator score says."]},
{heading:"Lipids and fatty liver",text:["High triglycerides, low HDL, abnormal LDL-related measures, and metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease can accompany excess adiposity. These conditions may be silent without laboratory tests or imaging.","Absence of symptoms should not be treated as evidence that metabolic markers are normal."]},
{heading:"Sleep apnea",text:["Obstructive sleep apnea is more common with obesity and can contribute to daytime sleepiness, hypertension, and cardiovascular strain. Loud snoring, witnessed breathing pauses, and excessive daytime sleepiness warrant appropriate evaluation.","A comorbidity score does not diagnose sleep apnea."]},
{heading:"Mobility and osteoarthritis",text:["Higher body mass can increase mechanical load on weight-bearing joints and may worsen pain or mobility limitations, although joint symptoms have many causes. Functional impact is an important outcome even when laboratory cardiometabolic markers are acceptable.","Track what a person can do comfortably, not only scale weight."]},
{heading:"Risk changes are multi-factorial",text:["Weight change can influence risk, but smoking, physical activity, sleep, medications, genetics, age, diet quality, and existing disease also matter. Improvements in blood pressure, glucose control, fitness, or sleep can be meaningful even when weight changes slowly.","Use trends in several health markers rather than one score as the only definition of progress."]},
{heading:"When to seek clinical assessment",text:["Chest pain, severe shortness of breath, fainting, symptoms of very high glucose, or other acute concerns require medical attention rather than calculator interpretation. For ongoing risk, clinicians can integrate history, examination, medications, labs, and validated risk tools.","Use this calculator for education and discussion, not for diagnosing or ruling out comorbid disease."]},
]},
"charitable-giving-tax-calculator":{title:"Charitable Giving Tax Calculator Guide",sections:[
{heading:"Tax benefit versus charitable intent",text:["A charitable deduction can reduce the after-tax cost of an eligible gift, but the deduction is not a dollar-for-dollar reimbursement. The donor gives away the asset and may receive a tax benefit based on deduction eligibility and marginal tax effects.","Choose the gift for philanthropic reasons first, then evaluate the most tax-efficient way to fund it."]},
{heading:"Itemizing matters",text:["For U.S. federal income tax, charitable deductions generally matter when deductible itemized expenses exceed the standard deduction, subject to current law and exceptions. A donor who does not itemize may receive little or no federal income-tax benefit from an otherwise qualified gift under ordinary rules.","Compare the tax return with and without the contribution rather than multiplying every gift by a tax bracket automatically."]},
{heading:"Qualified organizations",text:["Deductibility depends on the recipient and type of payment. Gifts to qualified charitable organizations can differ from payments to individuals, political organizations, crowdfunding campaigns, or purchases where the donor receives goods or services.","Verify an organization’s current tax status when the deduction is important."]},
{heading:"Cash contributions",text:["Cash gifts are straightforward to value, but documentation and percentage-of-income limitations can still apply. Bank records, receipts, and written acknowledgments become important at statutory thresholds.","A calculator can estimate tax effect but cannot create the substantiation required for a deduction."]},
{heading:"Appreciated securities",text:["Donating eligible long-term appreciated securities directly can potentially provide a charitable deduction based on applicable value rules while avoiding realization of embedded capital gain that would occur in a sale, subject to limitations.","Compare donating the asset directly with selling it and donating after-tax cash."]},
{heading:"Capital-gain property limits",text:["Deduction limits can differ for cash and capital-gain property and by recipient organization. Excess eligible contributions may be carried forward under applicable rules.","A simple percentage cap should be checked against the current tax year and the exact property/organization combination."]},
{heading:"Bunching contributions",text:["Donors near the itemization threshold sometimes combine several years of planned giving into one tax year. The larger deduction may make itemizing beneficial that year while the donor uses the standard deduction in surrounding years.","Bunching changes timing, not the underlying charitable budget."]},
{heading:"Donor-advised funds",text:["A donor-advised fund can allow a donor to claim a deduction when an irrevocable contribution is made to the sponsoring organization and recommend grants to operating charities later, subject to the fund’s rules.","The deduction year and the year the ultimate charity receives a grant may therefore differ."]},
{heading:"Qualified charitable distributions",text:["Eligible IRA owners may be able to make qualified charitable distributions directly from an IRA under current rules. A QCD is not simply an itemized charitable deduction; it can affect how IRA distributions enter income.","Do not model a QCD by applying the ordinary cash-deduction formula without checking current eligibility and limits."]},
{heading:"Using the calculator",text:["Estimate the gift, identify whether it is cash or property, determine likely itemization, apply only the deduction rules the calculator actually models, and compare after-tax cost across realistic strategies. State taxes, AGI limits, carryforwards, valuation, private-foundation rules, and substantiation can change the result.","For a large or complex gift, verify current IRS rules and coordinate tax and charitable planning before transferring the asset."]},
]},}